All,

There has been some confusion on the terminology related to various documen=
ts needed for customers.  This is in part because their isn't necessarily o=
ne name to call a document and the same name can be used for different thin=
gs.  Following are the names we will use.  The customers can use other term=
s.  You will have to translate into our terms for efficient and accurate ac=
tion.

Never just ask for submittals.  Be specific about which of these documents =
are needed.  Usually the Certification Letter and Engineering Data Sheets a=
re sufficient.

Before you request the information, get the customer to be specific about w=
hat he needs.  He may use different terminology than we do so you will have=
 to determine what are the information pieces he expects to see in the docu=
ment and then translate it into one of the following items.

Certification Letter:  This is usually a one page document on Harco letter =
head that addresses a whole product line and the standards/listings which w=
e certify it complies with.  It can also address an issue like certifying t=
o domestic manufacture of a product or product line(s).  It never includes =
engineering details like drawings of the products, dimensions of the produc=
t, pictures of the product, or installation instructions.

Engineering Data Sheets:  This is usually a multipage document for a produc=
t line and each configuration with the basic dimensions of interest to the =
typical user.  Usually there will be a citation of the industry standards t=
hat apply.  The term "cut sheet" usually refers to an engineering data shee=
t.  One would clarify with the customer if he wants a drawing showing dimen=
sional information of the product.

Installation Instructions:  This can be a separate sheet dedicated to insta=
llation or it can be incorporated in a product flyer or product brochure.

Brochure:  This is a multipage document that is oriented towards the sellin=
g of a product line or product.  It can contain a citation of the industry =
standards met, engineering data, suggested specifications, and installation=
 instructions.  Some of these elements may not be present.  It has pictures=
 or graphics of the product  and sometimes of the product in use.

Flyer:  This is a one or two page document (fits on one sheet of paper) tha=
t is oriented towards the selling of a more limited set of products.  It ca=
n contain a citation of the industry standards met, engineering data, sugge=
sted specification and installation instructions.  Some of these elements m=
ay not be present however.  It has pictures or graphics of the product  and=
 sometimes of the product in use.

Price Sheet:  This is a document that contains the items in a product line =
and their part number, list pricing, and other information.

Catalog:  This is a binder of documents relevant to multiple product lines =
that are sold into a market such as the Irrigation Market and Waterworks Ma=
rket.  It contains price sheets, brochures, and flyers.

Detail:  This is an engineering drawing made in a CAD file that a customer =
can use in his drawings or models.

Suggested Specification:  This is a statement of our product in such a way =
that a customer can insert it into his "specification", "design requirement=
s", or some other document that tells his contractors what they have to use=
 in the project.  Consultants and engineers often ask us to draft something=
 for them.  We often have these incorporated in our Brochures or Flyers but=
 not always.

Net Price:  This is the price we charge a customer for an item.  It is some=
times referred to as a price or a net.  This is not a cost although a custo=
mer may ask for his cost.  If you want a net price don't ask for a cost.

List Price:  This is the list price number showing in our price sheets and =
in our system to which we apply a multiplier to determine a customer's net =
price.

Cost:  This is our cost to produce an item.  Regional Sales Managers may on=
ly be advised of this number by Mike Freidman or John Riordan.  Do not ask =
for a cost when you want a Net Price or a List Price.

Multiplier:  This is the number multiplied times the list price to get a cu=
stomer's net price.  This is not a discount.  When a customer asks for his =
discount do not just tell him his multiplier.  Tell him "your multiplier is=
 .xy."  If you are not specific that the number you give him is a multiplie=
r he may process it as discount.  For instance a .30 meant to be interprete=
d as a multiplier if interpreted as a discount would be 30% off the list pr=
ice not .30 times the list price.  These are vastly different net prices.

There is more I am sure...

